## Calculation of RSET
This section outlines the assumptions and calculation method used to determine the RSET (i.e. the time from ignition to final occupant escape).
### Time to Detection
It is reasonable to expect that once fitted out, the warehouse will be fully covered by an alarm and detection system which includes detection within the warehouse. In the absence of other data, and to account for a range of possible detection systems, a time to detection of {{ detection_time }} seconds is conservatively assumed. This is considered to be a conservative assumption given the {{ growth_rate_label | lower }} growth rate of the fire, which would produce a large amount of smoke and would likely activate the detection system long before this time.
!! Check the detection assumptions against any proposed detection system for the building.
### Time to Alarm
It is reasonable to expect that once fitted out, the warehouse will be fully covered by an alarm and detection system which includes detection within the warehouse. On this basis, and assuming that the warehouse does not operate an investigation period, a time for the alarm to activate of 0 seconds has been assumed. This is in accordance with the guidance in PD 7974-6[8].
### Pre-movement Time
{% if pre_movement_is_default %}
A pre-movement time of 180 seconds has been assumed. This figure has been taken from guidance given in Table E.2 of PD 7974-6[8] and is the 99th percentile value for a space where the occupants are familiar with the layout of the building and “standard” management procedures are in place.
{% else %}
A pre-movement time of {{ pre_movement_time }} seconds has been assumed.
!! The pre-movement time is not the 180 s default from Table E.2 of PD 7974-6. Justify the value used.
{% endif %}
### Travel Time
As per CIBSE Guide E[7], {{ walking_speed }} m/s can be taken as an average speed for persons walking on flat surfaces. The maximum direct travel distance{% if fitout_known %} (with the indicative fit-out layout){% endif %} is {{ max_travel_distance }}m. As such, the travel time is taken to be {{ travel_time }} seconds.
### Queueing Time
The exit widths and maximum capacities have been sized using the guidance in BS 9999[1]. As noted in BS 7974-6 (Table G.4[8]), the maximum design sustained flow capacity implicit in the Approved Document B[3] guidance for a population in excess of 220 persons is 1.33 persons/m/s. {{ door_sentence }} Exit routes shared with the offices and secondary staircases are discounted.
